As it can be seen, the opaque appearance of hazardous raw
samples turned clear after the treatment irrespective of the
initial turbidity and the bioﬂocculant. In all cases, an eﬃcient
separation was attained, where chitosan led to an improved
sediment consolidation as compared to BSF, namely, BSF
formed ﬂocs containing higher percentage of adsorbed water.
This result was expected for chitosan; nevertheless, it was
conveniently surprising for the byproduct BSF used in the
direct ﬂocculation. For CCM samples, additional contaminants
could be helping in the ﬂocculation process when BSF is
